    <!-- T-CD-CRASH (FR-F §9): hide the top-level "Connected devices" entry.
         Root cause: the Connected Devices dashboard (ConnectedDeviceDashboard
         Fragment + PreviouslyConnectedDeviceDashboardFragment) constructs a
         LocalBluetoothManager via BluetoothAdapter.getDefaultAdapter() at
         fragment onCreate(). With the BT HAL excised (bt-excised.mk) +
         handheld_core_hardware override removing FEATURE_BLUETOOTH,
         getDefaultAdapter() returns null and the manager NPEs, crashing
         Settings when the user taps "Connected devices". The BT-aware
         controllers DO feature-gate individual rows, but the parent fragment
         eagerly initialises the BluetoothManager before the per-controller
         availability check runs — so hiding the rows is insufficient.
         Hiding the top-level entry (TopLevelConnectedDevicesPreference
         Controller.getAvailabilityStatus() reads this bool and returns
         UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE when false) is the cleanest fix and removes the
         crash surface entirely. NFC/USB device rows are reached via the
         System UI that already feature-gates them. -->
    <bool name="config_show_top_level_connected_devices">false</bool>

    <!-- T-W2-I2-FP: fingerprint HAL excision grace layer.
         Defence-in-depth: the fingerprint HAL feature XML
         (android.hardware.fingerprint.prebuilt.xml) is dropped in
         feature-excised/fp-excised.mk, so hasSystemFeature(FEATURE_FINGERPRINT)
         already returns false and Settings' biometric controllers hide the
         fingerprint preference at runtime. No Settings-specific config_fingerprint
         boolean exists to override (verified: packages/apps/Settings gates
         fingerprint UI on PackageManager.FEATURE_FINGERPRINT, not a config bool).
         This file documents the contract; the real hiding is the runtime feature
         check. Face biometrics intentionally NOT touched (out of scope). -->

